5. Develop a contiguity-based spatial weights matrix of your choosing (i.e., rook or queen)

    5.1. Row-standardize the W
```{r}

# Re-project to north american equidistant conic
JoinedData.projected <- JoinedData %>% sf::st_transform(., "ESRI:102010")

# make the neighborhood
nb <- spdep::poly2nb(JoinedData.projected, queen = TRUE)

# Assign weights using style W
lw <- nb2listw(nb, style="W", zero.policy=TRUE)

```
    
    5.2. Plot a histogram of the number of neighbors
```{r}

# Find the count of neighbors in the dataset
neighbors <- attr(lw$weights,"comp")$d 

# Plot it
hist(neighbors)


```
  
    5.3. Calculate the average number of neighbors
```{r}

mean(neighbors)

```
  
    5.4. Make a Moran Plot
```{r}

moran.plot(JoinedData.projected$PntPersonalCom, lw, zero.policy=TRUE, plot=TRUE)


```


6. Repeat #5 (and 5.1 - 5.4) above with a W developed using the IDW method. You will need to investigate the `spdep` documentation to find the correct method/function.
```{r}

# Make sure polygons are in right projection
JoinedData.projected <- JoinedData %>% sf::st_transform(., "ESRI:102010")

# Get the centroid of the counties - get the coords - & turn it into a DF
coords <- data.frame(st_coordinates(st_centroid(st_geometry(JoinedData.projected)))) 

# Convert the coords into a sf class so that it can be used in the nb2listwdist function
SF_coords <- st_as_sf(coords, coords = c("X", "Y"), crs = 102010) #

# make the neighborhood
nb_IDW <- spdep::poly2nb(JoinedData.projected, queen = TRUE)

# Assign weights using style W and IDW
lw_IDW <- nb2listwdist(neighbours = nb_IDW, x = SF_coords, type = 'idw', style = 'W', zero.policy=TRUE)

# Find the count of neighbors in the dataset
neighbors_IDW <- attr(lw_IDW$weights,"comp")$d 

# Plot amount of neighbors
hist(neighbors_IDW)

mean(neighbors_IDW)


moran.plot(JoinedData.projected$PntPersonalCom, lw_IDW, zero.policy=TRUE, plot=TRUE)


```


## Questions:

1. Describe in your own words how Moran’s I is calculated
Moran's I is calculated by comparing the values of locations near each other based on their proximity. If the values are similar then there is a positive spatial autocorrelation and it is measured between -1 and 1. The calculation also takes into account the overall average value across the dataset and the total number of locations. 


2. Describe in your own words: what is a spatially-lagged variable?
A spatially lagged variable measures the weighted average of surrounding neighbor's values. It is helpful in capturing spatial trends instead of relying on a single value to be representative of an area. 


3. How does your analysis in this lab (as simple as it is) differ by how you have formalized W (e.g., space, neighbors) in two diﬀerent methods? How might it affect analysis?
In the first method, the neighbors were defined as any that were touching the polygon. Then, these neighbors all had equal weight/importance. In the IDW method the neighbors were defined the same, but the closer the coordinates of the centroid were the more weight and importance the neighbor had. This method more closely follows the rule that objects that are closer together have more influence than those farther apart. 


4. What does it mean if an observation falls in the “H-L” quadrant? Why might it be useful to detect such occurances?
An observation in the H-L quadrant has a high value and is surrounded by lower values. Detecting these occurrences could be very helpful in decision-making. For example, if a high-crime area is surrounded by low-crime areas there may be important contributing factors to look at in the H-L observation.






